= Page format =

'''A BITwiki article should explain its subject clearly, support consequential claims with inspectable sources, and expose meaningful qualifications without requiring the reader to understand the wiki's internal architecture.''' Page format is the editorial arrangement that makes those goals possible.

A page is a coherent knowledge object first. Semantic fields, [[BITwiki:Book Matter|Book Matter]], templates, and transclusion help organize it, but none should substitute for an accurate and readable account.

== The subject determines the structure ==

BITwiki is a broad interdisciplinary encyclopedia. Mathematics, biology, medicine, philosophy, history, engineering, linguistics and other fields need different methods of exposition. An article does not need to invent a computational mechanism to belong; it should accurately represent its actual subject.

A mathematical theorem may benefit from a formal statement, proof, examples and limitations. An organism may need taxonomy, anatomy, ecology and evolutionary relationships. A historical event may require chronology, sources, competing accounts and consequences. A software system may need purpose, implementation, versions and verified behavior.

'''Use the structure the subject requires.''' A uniform checklist should not force empty sections, premature conclusions or invented explanations.

== Identify one canonical subject ==

The article title should name the actual subject rather than a category, portal, imagined computational equivalent or incidental aspect. '''One principal subject per article''' does not mean that an entire wider topic must be represented by only one canonical article.

Before starting or replacing an article, determine:

# Whether the subject already has a canonical page, redirect or closely overlapping treatment.
# What distinguishes it from neighboring concepts or artifacts.
# Whether the proposed title represents a phenomenon, theory, method, system, person, publication or another type of subject.
# What evidence or argument supports the substantial claims.
# Whether the material should form a complete article, improve an existing article, or remain a section within one.

A scientific phenomenon is not the same thing as a mathematical model of the phenomenon. A technical protocol is not the same thing as a particular implementation. The page should preserve such distinctions and link the related subjects where useful. See [[BITwiki:Ontology]] and [[BITwiki:Entity types]].

== An effective article opening ==

A reader should quickly learn:

* '''What it is:''' a precise, intelligible identification or definition.
* '''Why it matters:''' significance to inquiry, practice, history or human understanding.
* '''What makes it distinctive:''' the key property, process, problem or disagreement that warrants a separate explanation.
* '''How securely it is understood:''' qualification where a definition, explanation or result is contested.

For example, an article on [[Cell membrane]] should first identify the membrane's biological role before introducing specialist transport models. An article on [[Causality]] should distinguish causation from correlation while acknowledging that philosophical accounts of causation differ.

Avoid opening with maintenance status, repository history, migration decisions, workflow identifiers or declarations that the page passed validation. Such information may belong in change history or project documentation; it does not teach the reader the subject.

== Organizing the body ==

A commonly useful—but optional—sequence is:

{| class="wikitable"
! Section function !! Question it answers
|-
| '''Overview or definition''' || What is this?
|-
| '''Scope and context''' || Where does the term apply, and what meanings or boundaries are relevant?
|-
| '''Core concepts, structure or mechanism''' || How is the subject organized or explained, when a mechanism is appropriate?
|-
| '''Methods, examples or history''' || How is the concept investigated, illustrated or situated in time?
|-
| '''Applications and implications''' || What does understanding the subject enable, or what follows from it?
|-
| '''Evidence, limitations and disagreement''' || Which claims are supported, contested or limited to particular conditions?
|-
| '''References and related reading''' || Where can the reader check and pursue the account?
|}

Some subjects warrant a glossary, timeline, mathematical formalism, datasets, diagrams, implementation notes, comparisons or case studies. [[BITwiki:Book Matter]] names these distinct intellectual functions without requiring every one to become a separate page or template.

Sections should be substantial and useful. Combining two roles is often better than creating empty headings merely for structural uniformity.

== Claims and source discipline ==

An article should distinguish observation, mathematical derivation, documented history, reasoned interpretation and speculative proposal. Where the inference matters, explain how the sources support it rather than attaching a bibliography that does not correspond to the prose.

* Support important empirical and historical claims with appropriate identifiable sources.
* Cite specific works, passages, datasets, tests or records where practical.
* Attribute disputed or interpretive positions accurately and in proportion to their support.
* State meaningful limitations, versions, populations and assumptions.
* Use dates for claims that can change.
* Do not represent a model, a plausible mechanism or a source quotation as direct proof of a stronger claim.
* Do not invent citations, quantitative confidence or implementation results.

A well-supported subject can contain disputed explanations. A page-level epistemic label must not erase this difference. See [[BITwiki:Evidence]], [[BITwiki:Claim validation]], [[BITwiki:Epistemic status]] and [[BITwiki:Uncertainty]].

== Reading depths represent one subject ==

BITwiki uses three source-level presentations for suitable public concepts:

{| class="wikitable"
! Surface !! Editorial role
|-
| '''Simple''' — <code>Title/Simple</code> || An accurate low-prerequisite orientation using clear terms and examples.
|-
| '''Core''' — <code>Title</code> || The canonical general account, intelligible without forcing the reader into a specialist treatment.
|-
| '''Technical''' — <code>Title/Technical</code> || Formalism, detailed methods, implementation considerations, standards and advanced limitations when meaningful.
|}

These are pedagogical presentations of the same subject, not three competing ontological identities. Source-level pages commonly use [[Template:Reading depth]] for navigation.

Simple must not turn uncertain statements into facts. Technical must not contradict the Core definition. The unsuffixed Core page owns the canonical subject treatment; derivative presentations ordinarily should not repeat identity properties solely to satisfy a template.

'''Frontier research is not a fourth mandatory reading-depth page.''' Open questions, emerging hypotheses and unsettled evidence can be addressed explicitly within the appropriate Core or Technical account.

Not every topic requires the same number of depth pages. Creating a nearly empty Technical page for a subject without meaningful advanced treatment adds maintenance without improving understanding. See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]].

== Semantic identity is supplementary ==

For a page using [[Template:Knowledge object]], the current source-controlled invocation accepts:

* <code>entity_type</code> — one of the defined primary types;
* <code>domain</code> — the appropriate subject field or fields;
* <code>status</code> — a defensible page-level epistemic standing;
* <code>provenance</code> — a concise origin or transformation description.

'''The four parameters are optional.''' Omit a value rather than invent a type, domain, source history, or page-wide epistemic standing. Unregistered entity types and domains are retained for explicit vocabulary review rather than blocking the subject. An invalid asserted epistemic status still produces a validation error.

This is a narrow metadata interface, not a complete article format, an admission certificate, or a validation of every sentence. An article may need nuanced claim-level discussion beyond its page-level standing. Where a meaningful page-level label cannot be justified, omit it and explain any significant claim-level distinctions in the article.

The compiler checks supplied metadata: it flags unregistered types/domains for review and rejects an explicitly invalid epistemic standing. Optional missing values are not errors. It does not audit factual accuracy, citation quality, live deployment, or semantic assertions in every page. See [[BITwiki:Semantic properties]].

== Linking and structured relations ==

Ordinary MediaWiki links should connect concepts naturally within the text. Readers should not have to decode graph terminology to follow an explanation.

Typed semantic properties are different: <code>Implements</code>, <code>Part of</code>, <code>Depends on</code>, <code>Supports</code>, <code>Contradicts</code>, <code>Refines</code>, and <code>Derived from</code> state specific relationships. They require justification and appropriate direction; do not create them merely because two pages mention each other.

Choose the article boundary according to the claims, sources, scope, duplication risk, reader needs and connections involved:

* '''One article, several perspectives:''' when interpretations address the same principal subject and can be compared with sufficient context in coherent sections, present and attribute them together, including their supporting and conflicting evidence.
* '''Several related articles:''' when a model, disciplinary analysis or distinct claim has an independently sourced scope that warrants its own maintained account, give it a canonical subject and link it to the related topic article. Avoid repeating the same exposition merely to multiply perspectives; use justified semantic relations only for claims their definitions actually support.

[[BITwiki:Navigation]] explains portals, categories and indices. These are discovery tools, not a replacement for accurate subjects or an ontology. See [[BITwiki:Relationships]].

== Reusable content and composition ==

Use [[BITwiki:Book Matter]] to identify what explanatory, evidentiary, analytical, procedural or reference function a passage performs. A presentation form (infobox, timeline, diagram or index) may carry several functions; it does not require a separate ontological type or page.

The default is a coherent article with sections written for its own subject. '''Keep one-use material inline; link to distinct canonical subjects; transclude only when a separately maintained component has demonstrable reuse value.''' A shorter contextual summary and a link are often more intelligible than embedding a verbatim fragment from a different argument.

When material is legitimately reused, the article must remain coherent for its reader. Reused matter retains its original evidence, limitations and scope, and the host explains why it applies. A shared paragraph must not inadvertently assign a source fragment's semantic annotations to the whole host. The choice to extract and review material belongs to [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]]; wikitext syntax and practical verification belong to [[Help:Transclusion]].

Simple, Core and Technical surfaces may use different wording to teach the same subject. Shared factual accuracy and epistemic qualifications matter more than literal prose reuse; direct transclusion of a Core passage into a Simple page is not automatically pedagogically suitable.

== Different page roles ==

This format chiefly guides public subject articles in Main. Other pages have different jobs:

* A '''BITwiki project page''' documents governance, editorial methods or architecture without pretending to be an encyclopedia article.
* A '''Template''' defines reusable rendering or annotation behavior.
* A '''Property''' documents a semantic attribute or relation.
* A '''Portal''' is a reader-facing navigation view, not an additional kind of knowledge object.
* A '''Help''' page provides practical instructions.
* A '''research request''' records an identified coverage need but is not a finished article.

Page role, entity type, category and domain should remain distinct. Where an article is revised or merged, preserve meaningful references, links, attribution and source history rather than simply changing the title.

== Editorial review before publication ==

Review a proposed page through the substance, not just its compliance with structure:

# '''Identity:''' Is the subject distinct and accurately named?
# '''Explanation:''' Can the intended reader understand the main claim and its significance?
# '''Evidence:''' Do the cited materials support what is actually asserted?
# '''Boundaries:''' Are disagreement, uncertainty, scope and time identified when relevant?
# '''Composition:''' Do headings, media and reusable components aid comprehension?
# '''Relations:''' Are links and semantic claims accurate rather than decorative?
# '''Depth:''' Are Simple, Core and Technical presentations mutually consistent where they exist?
# '''Maintenance:''' Can future editors trace the provenance and update the account coherently?

Passing repository validation does not establish editorial acceptance or a successful deployment to the live MediaWiki installation. See [[BITwiki:Epistemic audit]].

